[GROUP - PRO Version Only]
{\rtf1\ansi\fs22
The \b PRO Version \b0 of the DeBloater includes several advanced features not available in the FREE Version.  
These features are designed for technicians, system builders, and IT professionals who maintain multiple PCs 
and require deeper insight, repeatable workflows, and more powerful automation.  \par
\par
Current PRO features include:  \par
   •   Scanning \b Windows STARTUP Programs\b0, \par
       with Advice for each item found, \par
   •   Scanning \b INSTALLED Programs\b0, \par
       with Advice for each program found, \par
   •   Using \b SAVED Options\b0, created on this or other machines, \par
   •   Enabling \b Windows Activation\b0, \par
       using a saved Activation Key (optionally encrypted), \par
   •   A PRO‑only Button to \b SAVE OPTIONS Used\b0,  \par
       for reuse across multiple PCs.  \par
\par
In addition, several \b Future PRO Features\b0 are planned.  
These are documented here as an aide‑memoire for ongoing development:  \par

\b Preview Mode (Dry‑Run)\b0 — allows the User to see exactly what would be changed, 
disabled, or removed before any actions are performed.  
Useful for high‑risk or unknown systems.  \par

\b Export Task Log to File\b0 — saves the Task Log as a text or RTF file for audit trails, 
ticketing systems, compliance documentation, or before/after comparisons.  \par

\b Machine Summary Snapshot\b0 — generates a concise overview of the system, 
including Windows edition, activation status, CPU/RAM, disk usage, number of STARTUP items, 
and number of INSTALLED Programs.  Helps technicians assess system health before making changes.  \par

\b Scheduled Task Scan\b0 — enumerates all Scheduled Tasks on the system, including OEM, vendor, 
updater, telemetry, and background maintenance tasks.  
Provides Advice indicating whether each task is essential, optional, safe to disable, 
or associated with OEM bloat or unwanted activity.  
Intended to complement the STARTUP and INSTALLED Program scans.  \par

\b Windows Services Scan\b0 — lists all non‑Microsoft services installed on the system, including OEM, 
vendor, telemetry, updater, and leftover services from previously removed software.  
Provides Advice indicating whether each service is essential, optional, or safe to disable.  \par

\b Windows Store Apps Scan\b0 — identifies installed and provisioned AppX packages, including OEM‑bundled 
apps, trialware, promotional apps, and unnecessary Microsoft Store components.  
Provides Advice on whether each app is essential, optional, or removable.  \par

\b Browser Extensions Scan\b0 — lists installed extensions for major browsers (Chrome, Edge, Firefox), 
highlighting OEM‑added items, telemetry extensions, and potentially unwanted add‑ons.  
Provides Advice on safety, usefulness, and performance impact.  \par

\b Windows Features Scan\b0 — enumerates optional Windows Features (DISM components) enabled on the system, 
such as legacy components, SMB1, XPS Viewer, and Hyper‑V modules.  
Provides Advice on whether each feature is required, optional, or unnecessary.  \par

\b Windows Update Status Summary\b0 — reports last successful update, number of pending updates, 
and whether update components appear healthy.  
Helps diagnose systems with update failures or stalled update cycles.  \par

\b User Profiles Summary\b0 — lists all local user profiles, including size, age, and last‑used time.  
Useful for identifying abandoned or oversized profiles that may affect disk usage or login speed.  \par

\b Disk Health Summary (SMART)\b0 — provides a simple health overview of attached storage devices, 
including SMART status, power‑on hours, and reallocated sector count.  
Helps determine whether system slowness is hardware‑related.  \par

\b Event Log Quick Scan\b0 — provides a brief count of critical errors, warnings, and repeated issues 
from key Windows logs.  
Useful for identifying underlying system problems before cleanup.  \par

\b Silent Mode / Command‑Line Mode\b0 — allows automated or unattended execution using a saved Options Set.  
Intended for deployment scripts, imaging workflows, and large‑scale rollouts.  \par

\b OEM Bloat Score\b0 — provides a simple numerical score indicating the amount of OEM‑installed software, 
background updaters, and unnecessary components detected on the system.  
Useful for comparing machines or demonstrating improvements after cleanup.  \par

\par
These Future PRO Features are not yet implemented, but are included here to preserve the design intent and 
long‑term roadmap for the PRO Version.  \par

}

