[OPTION - Turn BitLocker OFF]
{\rtf1\ansi\fs22\b0
BitLocker encrypts your drives so that if you lose your PC, or someone steals it, they cannot access the data stored on them. However, it does not protect your information against hackers, malware, or anyone who already knows your Windows password and can log in normally.\par
\par
For most home users, BitLocker provides limited real-world benefit and can sometimes cause inconvenience, especially if hardware changes or boot issues occur. Unless you have highly sensitive data that must remain protected even if the device is physically stolen, BitLocker is often unnecessary.\par
\par
If this item is \b checked \b0 and BitLocker is currently \b ON\b0, we will turn it off immediately. Windows will then decrypt your drive in the background — this can take up to several hours on a large drive. Your PC remains fully usable throughout.\par
\par
If you un-check this item, your system will not be altered.\par
\b    •   \b0 If BitLocker is currently ON, it will remain ON. \par
\b    •   \b0 If BitLocker is currently OFF, it will remain OFF.\par
\par
If BitLocker is currently OFF and you want it ON, you will HAVE to enable it through Windows itself,
as your whole drive will have to be encrypted.\par
\par
\b Important: \b0 Windows 11 can silently re-enable Device Encryption after updates or when signed in with a Microsoft Account — without warning you. To permanently block this, we also set a Windows Registry key that prevents Windows from doing so without your knowledge.\par
}

