[GROUP - Simplify the Taskbar]
{\rtf1\ansi\fs22\b0
\b Please Note: \b0 Microsoft’s security does not permit anything to be pinned to, 
or removed from the Taskbar, unless it is done by YOU, the User, 
through the standard Windows interface.  Therefore, we cannot do it for you.  
We can offer Advice on which Apps to KEEP or Remove, but you will have to do it for yourself.  \par
\par
Simply point at any Option you are interested in.  
You will then, receive full instructions on how to do it yourself, here in the Advice Panel.  
This can then be printed, one at a time, or appended to you own WORD document, for later attention.  \par
\par
\b Once upon a time \b0 , the only applications that appeared in the Taskbar, were ones that were currently running.  
This was to enable you to switch between them, easily.  \par
\par
These days, however, you can pin Applications permanently to the Taskbar.  
The problem with this, is that you cannot tell which are open, and which are not - simply by looking at the Taskbar.  
We therefore suggest that you restrict pinning Apps to the Taskbar that you use frequently and will open with a single click.  \par
\par
For most people this will be the Applications shown.  
For other applications that have Documents associated with them that you use fairly frequently, like Microsoft WORD, 
for instance, it is just as easy to use the Start Menu, anyway.  
For, if you pinned WORD to the Taskbar, and opened it from there, you'd still have open the Document you want.  \par
\par
By comparison, clicking the Start Menu once, then clicking the Document you want, 
is an absolute breeze - as most of the time it's a recent Document!  
So, there is no point in it permanently occupying space on the Taskbar.  \par
\par

}

