[GROUP - Privacy && Telemetry]
{\rtf1\ansi
\par
\fs22 Windows 11 collects a significant amount of diagnostic and behavioural data. Some of this helps Microsoft improve Windows, but much of it is used for advertising, personalisation, and product recommendations.\par
\par
\b\fs20 This group allows you to reduce or eliminate the most intrusive data collection.\b0\fs22\par
\par
These options do not affect system stability, and most users will benefit from disabling them.\par
\par
}
[SELECT - ALL]

[OPTION - Remove Advertising ID]
{\rtf1\ansi\fs22\b0
The Advertising ID is used by apps and websites to track your behaviour and personalise ads.  
Disabling it does not remove advertising from Windows.  It only stops ads being tailored to your activity.  
You will still see ads, but they will be generic rather than targeted.\par
\par
Windows advertising comes from several different sources, including the Start Menu, the lock screen, Widgets, 
Microsoft Store apps, and system suggestions.  This option only affects personalised advertising.  
Other types of Windows advertising are controlled by separate settings, which are covered in later options.\par
\par
If you use a Microsoft Account, some online services may still personalise content based on your web activity.  
If you use a local account, disabling the Advertising ID prevents apps from tracking your behaviour entirely.  \par
\par
\b\fs20 Ad blockers cannot remove Windows‑built‑in advertising \b0,\par
but Options below can.  They are marked with: \par
 \b This option reduces or removes Windows‑built‑in advertising\b0 \par
}

[OPTION - Remove Tailored Experiences]
{\rtf1\ansi\fs22\b0
Tailored Experiences uses your diagnostic data to customise tips, ads, and recommendations across Windows.  
Disabling it does not remove advertising, but it prevents Windows from analysing how you use your device to influence what you see.\par
\par
This setting controls behaviour-based personalisation inside Windows itself.  
It does not affect web advertising or Microsoft Store apps, and it does not depend on the Advertising ID.\par
\par
If you use a Microsoft Account, some online services may still personalise content based on your web activity.  
If you use a local account, disabling Tailored Experiences prevents Windows from using your diagnostic data for any personalised suggestions.  \par
\par
\b\fs20 This option reduces or removes Windows‑built‑in advertising.\b0\fs22\par
}

[OPTION - Remove Inking and Typing Personalisation]
{\rtf1\ansi\fs22\b0
Windows can analyse your typing and handwriting to improve text suggestions and handwriting recognition.  
This data is processed in the cloud, and disabling this feature prevents Windows from sending your typing patterns 
or handwriting samples to Microsoft.\par
\par
Turning this off does not affect normal typing or the on‑screen keyboard.  It only disables cloud‑based learning and personalisation.  
If you do not use handwriting features, or if you prefer not to have your input data analysed, it is safe to disable this option.\par
\par
If you use a Microsoft Account, some personalisation may still occur in online services, 
but Windows will no longer upload typing or handwriting data from this device.  
If you use a local account, disabling this feature prevents Windows from collecting or analysing any inking or typing data.\par
}

[OPTION - Remove Location Tracking]
{\rtf1\ansi\fs22\b0
Location services allow Windows and apps to determine your approximate position using Wi‑Fi, network data, and optional device sensors.  
Disabling this prevents apps from accessing your location and stops Windows from storing or sharing location information.\par
\par
Turning this off does not affect core system functions, but apps that rely on location - such as Maps, Weather, 
or Find My Device - may show less accurate or less relevant information.  If you do not use location‑based features, 
disabling this option improves privacy with no noticeable impact.\par
\par
If you use a Microsoft Account, some online services may still estimate your location based on your IP address.  
If you use a local account, disabling Location Tracking prevents Windows and apps on this device from accessing or storing your location.\par
}

[OPTION - Remove Activity History]
{\rtf1\ansi\fs22\b0
Activity History stores a record of the apps you use, the files you open, and the websites you visit, 
and it can sync this information to your Microsoft Account.  
Disabling this prevents Windows from collecting this activity data and stops it from being uploaded to the cloud.\par
\par
Turning this off does not affect normal app usage or your ability to reopen recent files.  
It only disables the centralised activity log that Windows uses for features such as Timeline and cross‑device continuity.  
If you prefer not to have a record of your activity stored or synced, it is safe to disable this option.\par
\par
If you use a Microsoft Account, disabling Activity History prevents Windows from uploading your activity to your account.  
If you use a local account, Windows will not collect or store Activity History on this device at all.\par
}

[OPTION - Remove Feedback Frequency]
{\rtf1\ansi\fs22\b0
Windows occasionally prompts you to provide feedback about your experience.  
Reducing the feedback frequency prevents these pop‑ups from interrupting your work and stops Windows from requesting additional 
diagnostic information.\par
\par
Disabling or minimising feedback prompts does not affect system performance or functionality.  
It simply reduces the number of notifications Windows displays and prevents Windows from asking for optional feedback.\par
\par
If you use a Microsoft Account, feedback you choose to provide may still be associated with your account.  
If you use a local account, Windows will not link any feedback to an online identity, and reducing feedback frequency prevents most prompts from appearing.\par
}

[OPTION - Remove Diagnostic Data Upload]
{\rtf1\ansi\fs22\b0
Diagnostic data includes information about how Windows and apps perform on your device.  
When enabled, Windows can upload this data to Microsoft to help identify problems, improve features, and provide troubleshooting suggestions.  
Disabling this option prevents Windows from sending diagnostic data beyond what is required for basic security and reliability.\par
\par
Turning this off does not affect normal system operation.  It simply limits the amount of information Windows is allowed to upload.  
Features that rely on enhanced diagnostics, such as tailored troubleshooting or certain preview features, 
may provide less detailed recommendations.\par
\par
If you use a Microsoft Account, some online services may still collect diagnostic information related to those services.  
If you use a local account, disabling Diagnostic Data Upload prevents Windows from sending optional diagnostic data from this device.\par
}

[OPTION - Remove Cloud Content Suggestions]
{\rtf1\ansi\fs22\b0
Windows may suggest content from the cloud, including files, apps, and online recommendations based on how you use Microsoft services.  
These suggestions can appear in the Start Menu, File Explorer, and other parts of Windows.\par
\par
Disabling this option prevents Windows from analysing your cloud activity to generate personalised suggestions.  
It does not affect your ability to access cloud files or use services such as OneDrive, Outlook, or Microsoft Store apps.  
It simply stops Windows from using your cloud usage patterns to recommend content.\par
\par
If you use a Microsoft Account, some online services may still personalise content within the service itself.  
If you use a local account, disabling Cloud Content Suggestions prevents Windows from using any cloud‑based activity 
from this device for personalised recommendations.\par
\par
\b\fs20 This option reduces or removes Windows‑built‑in advertising.\b0\fs22\par
}

[OPTION - Remove Web Search in Start Menu]
{\rtf1\ansi\fs22\b0
When you search in the Start Menu, Windows may send your query to Bing to provide online results alongside local files and apps.  
This can include suggestions, web links, and other cloud‑based content.\par
\par
Disabling this option keeps all Start Menu searches local to your PC.  Windows will no longer send your search terms to Bing, 
and the Start Menu will only show results from your installed apps, settings, and local files.\par
\par
If you use a Microsoft Account, online services may still provide web results inside their own apps, but Start Menu searches will remain local.  
If you use a local account, disabling this option prevents Windows from sending any Start Menu search queries to the cloud.\par
\par
\b\fs20 This option reduces or removes Windows‑built‑in advertising.\b0\fs22\par
}

[OPTION - KEEP SmartScreen Filter]
{\rtf1\ansi\fs22\b0
SmartScreen protects you from malicious websites, unsafe downloads, and potentially harmful apps.  
It checks files and links against Microsoft’s security database to warn you before you open something dangerous.\par
\par
Although SmartScreen sends limited data to Microsoft for threat analysis, it provides significant security benefits 
and helps block common attacks.  Disabling it reduces your protection against phishing, malware, and untrusted downloads.\par
\par
We recommend keeping SmartScreen enabled unless you have a specific reason to disable it or you already use alternative security tools 
that provide equivalent protection.\par
}

[OPTION - Remove Microsoft Edge Telemetry]
{\rtf1\ansi\fs22\b0
Microsoft Edge, like Windows itself, collects and sends telemetry data to Microsoft.  
This is separate from Windows telemetry and operates independently - disabling Windows diagnostic data does not stop Edge from sending its own usage and performance data.\par
\par
Edge telemetry includes information about how you use the browser, which features you interact with, and performance metrics.  
While Microsoft states this data is anonymised, it is collected continuously in the background.\par
\par
Disabling Edge telemetry prevents the browser from sending this usage data to Microsoft.  
It does not affect browsing speed, security updates, or any browser features.\par
\par
\b Note:\b0  Edge may still send minimal data required for security features such as SmartScreen.  
This option reduces optional telemetry, not security-critical communication.\par
}

[OPTION - Remove Customer Experience Improvement Program]
{\rtf1\ansi\fs22\b0
The Customer Experience Improvement Program (CEIP) is a Microsoft scheme that collects anonymous data about how you use Windows and Microsoft software.  
This includes information about hardware configuration, feature usage patterns, and application behaviour.\par
\par
Microsoft uses this data to identify trends and improve future versions of Windows and its applications.  
Participation is technically voluntary, but CEIP is enabled by default and runs silently in the background through a series of scheduled tasks.\par
\par
Disabling CEIP stops these scheduled tasks from running and prevents Windows from collecting and uploading this usage data.  
It does not affect system performance, stability, or your ability to receive updates.\par
\par
\b Note:\b0  CEIP is separate from Windows diagnostic telemetry, which is controlled by the \b Remove Diagnostic Data Upload\b0  option above.  
Disabling both together provides the most comprehensive reduction in background data collection.\par
}


=======================================================================================================================
[GROUP - Performance Acceleration]
{\rtf1\ansi
\par
\fs22 Windows includes several hidden performance features that improve responsiveness, reduce UI latency, 
and optimise CPU scheduling. These options enable Microsoft’s internal Low Latency Profile (LLP), foreground scheduling boosts,
 and reduced background task throttling.\par
\par
\b\fs20 This group enables advanced performance acceleration features.\b0\fs22\par
All options are safe and reversible.\par
\par
}
[SELECT - ALL]

[OPTION - ENABLE Low Latency Profile (LLP)]
{\rtf1\ansi\fs22
Microsoft’s hidden Low Latency Profile (LLP), temporarily boosts CPU frequency during Start Menu, Search, and shell interactions.  
This improves responsiveness and reduces UI lag, especially on low-end or older hardware.  \par
\par
Requires KB5089573 or later.\par
}

[OPTION - ENABLE Scheduler Responsiveness Mode]
{\rtf1\ansi\fs22
Improves foreground application responsiveness by increasing foreground CPU quantum length and priority boost.  
This reduces input latency and improves UI smoothness, especially on older or heavily loaded systems.\par
\par
May slightly reduce background task performance.
}

[OPTION - ENABLE Reduced Background Task Latency]
{\rtf1\ansi\fs22
This works by disabling Windows background throttling mechanisms including Power Throttling, Background Access throttling, 
and EcoQoS.  This reduces latency for background tasks and improves overall system responsiveness.\par
\par
May increase battery usage on laptops.
}


=======================================================================================================================

[GROUP - Visual Effects]
{\rtf1\ansi
\par
\fs22 Windows 11 uses transparency, animations, and shadow effects throughout its interface.  On modern hardware these are barely noticeable.  On older or lower-specification machines they can cause sluggishness, delayed window responses, and reduced overall responsiveness.\par
\par
\b\fs20 This group allows you to disable visual effects that consume CPU and GPU resources without affecting functionality.\b0\fs22\par
All options are safe and reversible.\par
\par
}
[SELECT - ALL]

[OPTION - DISABLE Transparency Effects]
{\rtf1\ansi\fs22
Windows 11 applies a frosted-glass transparency effect to the Start Menu, Taskbar, Settings, and other windows.  This requires continuous GPU compositing to render the blurred backgrounds behind each transparent surface.\par
\par
On modern hardware this is negligible.  On older or integrated-graphics systems it can contribute to sluggish UI responses, particularly when opening the Start Menu or switching between windows.\par
\par
Disabling transparency replaces the frosted-glass effect with a solid background colour.  The interface looks slightly flatter but behaves identically in every other way.\par
\par
\b Note:\b0  This setting is per-user.  It applies to the current Windows user account only.\par
}

[OPTION - DISABLE Window Animations]
{\rtf1\ansi\fs22
Windows animates windows as they open, close, minimise, and maximise.  It also animates menus, tooltips, and other interface elements as they appear and disappear.  On fast hardware these transitions are smooth and barely perceptible.\par
\par
On slower or older machines, particularly those with limited RAM or integrated graphics, these animations add measurable delay to every window interaction.  Disabling them makes the interface feel noticeably more responsive - windows open and close instantly rather than fading or sliding into place.\par
\par
This is one of the single most effective visual changes for improving perceived performance on older hardware.\par
\par
\b Note:\b0  This setting is per-user.  It applies to the current Windows user account only.\par
}

=======================================================================================================================

[GROUP - Background Services]
{\rtf1\ansi
\par
\fs22 Windows 11 runs a number of background services that support features such as gaming, cloud sync, widgets, and content suggestions. Many users do not need these services, and disabling them can reduce resource usage and improve privacy.\par
\par
\b\fs20 This group allows you to disable non-essential background services.\b0\fs22\par
None of these changes affect core Windows functionality.\par
\par
}

[SELECT - ALL]

[OPTION - Remove Widgets Service]
{\rtf1\ansi\fs22
Widgets provide news, weather, sports, and other online content from the left-hand side of the Taskbar.  
They rely on background services that regularly connect to the internet to download updates and personalised information.\par
\par
If you do not use Widgets, removing the service reduces network usage and prevents Windows from displaying news feeds, 
promotional cards, or other online suggestions.  This also stops the Widgets panel from running in the background.\par
\par
Removing the Widgets service does not affect core system functions or the Taskbar itself.  
It simply disables the online content feed and the background processes that support it.\par
}

[OPTION - Remove Xbox Game Bar Service]
{\rtf1\ansi\fs22
The Xbox Game Bar provides screen recording, performance overlays, game capture tools, and social features for gamers.  
t also runs background services that support in‑game shortcuts and real‑time performance monitoring.\par
\par
If you do not play games on your PC, removing this service reduces background activity and frees system resources.  
This also prevents the Game Bar interface from appearing when gaming shortcuts are pressed.\par
\par
Removing the Xbox Game Bar service does not affect normal system operation or third‑party screen recording tools.  
It simply disables the built‑in gaming overlay and its supporting background processes.\par
}

[OPTION - Remove Xbox Live Auth Manager]
{\rtf1\ansi\fs22
The Xbox Live Auth Manager handles sign‑in and authentication for Xbox accounts.  
It is required only for Microsoft Store games that use Xbox services, achievements, cloud saves, or multiplayer features.\par
\par
If you do not use Xbox services or play games that rely on Xbox integration, it is safe to remove this service.  
Doing so reduces background activity and prevents Xbox‑related components from running unnecessarily.\par
\par
Removing the Xbox Live Auth Manager does not affect normal system operation or non‑Xbox games.  
It simply disables authentication features used by Xbox apps and Microsoft Store titles that depend on Xbox services.\par
}

[OPTION - Remove Xbox Accessory Management Service]
{\rtf1\ansi\fs22
The Xbox Accessory Management Service supports Xbox controllers and other Xbox‑branded hardware.  
It enables features such as accessory configuration, firmware updates, and device management for compatible peripherals.\par
\par
If you do not use Xbox controllers or other Xbox accessories, removing this service has no negative impact.  
Doing so reduces background activity and prevents Xbox‑related components from running unnecessarily.\par
\par
Removing the Xbox Accessory Management Service does not affect standard USB or Bluetooth controllers that do not rely on Xbox‑specific features.  
It simply disables management functions for Xbox‑branded hardware.\par
}

[OPTION - Remove Game DVR and Capture Service]
{\rtf1\ansi\fs22
Game DVR records gameplay and captures screenshots in the background.  
It supports features such as automatic recording, game clips, and performance overlays used by the Xbox Game Bar.\par
\par
If you do not record games or use the Game Bar’s capture tools, disabling this service prevents unnecessary background disk and CPU usage.  
This also stops Windows from creating automatic game clips while you play.\par
\par
Removing the Game DVR and Capture Service does not affect normal system operation or third‑party recording tools.  
It simply disables Windows’ built‑in game capture features and their associated background processes.\par
}

[OPTION - Remove OneDrive Auto-Start]
{\rtf1\ansi\fs22
Even if OneDrive is installed, against our advice, you may not want it running automatically.  
When enabled, OneDrive launches at startup, signs you in, and begins syncing files in the background.\par
\par
Disabling auto-start prevents OneDrive from launching in the background and syncing files without your knowledge.  
This reduces startup load and stops OneDrive from using network or disk activity unless you open it manually.\par
\par
Removing OneDrive auto-start does not uninstall OneDrive or affect your ability to use it.  
You can still open OneDrive at any time and sync files when you choose.\par
}
[OPTION - KEEP Windows Spotlight Service]
{\rtf1\ansi\fs22
Windows Spotlight downloads images, tips, and suggestions for the lock screen.  
It regularly updates the background with new photos and may display occasional informational or promotional content from Microsoft.\par
\par
If you prefer a static background, removing this service reduces network usage and prevents Spotlight from downloading new images or suggestions.  However, keeping it enabled allows Windows to refresh your lock screen with high‑quality images and curated content.\par
\par
We recommend keeping Windows Spotlight enabled if you enjoy the rotating lock screen images or want Windows to personalise 
the lock screen experience.  If you prefer a fixed background, it is safe to remove.\par
}

[OPTION - KEEP Windows Search Service]
{\rtf1\ansi\fs22
Windows Search indexes your files, apps, and settings to provide fast and accurate search results.  
It maintains a local index so that searches complete quickly without scanning your entire drive each time.\par
\par
Disabling this service can slow down file searches significantly, especially on systems with large drives or many documents.  
Without the index, Windows must search your files manually, which takes much longer.\par
\par
We recommend keeping Windows Search enabled unless you rarely use Windows search or rely exclusively on third‑party search tools.  
For most users, the performance benefits outweigh the background indexing activity.\par
}

[OPTION - Remove Suggested Actions Service]
{\rtf1\ansi\fs22
Suggested Actions analyses copied text, such as dates, phone numbers, or addresses, and offers contextual actions like creating calendar events 
or making calls.  It monitors clipboard activity to detect when these suggestions may be relevant.\par
\par
If you prefer not to have Windows analyse clipboard content, you may safely remove this service.  
Disabling it prevents Windows from scanning copied text and stops contextual suggestions from appearing.\par
\par
Removing the Suggested Actions Service does not affect normal clipboard functionality or third‑party clipboard tools.  
It simply disables Windows’ built‑in detection and suggestion features.\par
}
