[GROUP - System Experience]
{\rtf1\ansi\fs22 
Windows 11 includes a range of features designed to "enhance the experience" by offering suggestions, tips, promotions, and automated behaviours. Many users find these distracting or unnecessary.\par
\par
\b\fs20 This group allows you to disable Windows features that interrupt, recommend, or promote content.\b0\fs22\par
\par
These changes do not affect system stability.\par
\par
}
[SELECT - ALL]

[OPTION - Remove Tips and Suggestions]
{\rtf1\ansi\fs22
Windows occasionally displays tips, suggestions, and pop‑ups to guide you through features or highlight new capabilities.  These appear in the Taskbar, notifications, and various parts of the interface.\par
\par
While helpful for new users, many people find them distracting or intrusive.  They can interrupt your workflow and introduce unexpected prompts while you are using the system.\par
\par
Disabling them creates a quieter, more predictable experience.  Windows will no longer show feature suggestions or promotional tips while you work.\par
}

[OPTION - Remove Lock Screen Spotlight]
{\rtf1\ansi\fs22
Windows Spotlight downloads images and promotional content for the lock screen.\par
\par
If you prefer a static background without suggestions or ads, removing Spotlight is recommended.\par
}

[OPTION - Remove Start Menu Recommendations]
{\rtf1\ansi\fs22
The Start Menu includes a Recommendations section located directly below your pinned apps.  
This area may show recently opened files, recently installed apps, suggested apps, website links, or other activity‑based items.\par
\par
Many users expect this section to behave like a simple "recent files" list, but it often updates slowly and prioritises suggestions 
or promotional content instead of the files you actually used.  Because of this, the Recommendations area can feel inconsistent and 
not particularly useful.\par
\par
Removing Start Menu recommendations hides this entire section and keeps the Start Menu focused solely on your pinned apps.  
Windows will no longer display suggested or activity‑based content in the Start Menu.\par
}

[OPTION - Remove Suggested Apps]
{\rtf1\ansi\fs22
Windows may display suggested apps from the Microsoft Store in the Start Menu.  
These suggestions can appear as promotional tiles mixed into your pinned apps, or as recommended items in the Recommendations section.\par
\par
If you prefer a cleaner Start Menu without promotional content, you can disable these suggestions.  
Doing so prevents Windows from inserting Store app recommendations or advertising tiles.\par
}

[OPTION - Remove Suggested Content in Settings]
{\rtf1\ansi\fs22
The Settings app may display suggested content such as Microsoft account reminders, promotional messages, 
or recommendations for Microsoft services.  These appear at the top of various Settings pages and can include tips, offers, 
or prompts to use additional features.\par
\par
If you prefer a cleaner, distraction‑free Settings experience, you can disable these suggestions.  
Doing so removes promotional content and keeps the Settings app focused solely on configuration options.\par
}

[OPTION - Remove Automatic App Launching]
{\rtf1\ansi\fs22
Some apps may launch automatically after updates or during system startup.  
This can happen when apps register themselves to run in the background, reopen after system restarts, or relaunch following Store updates.\par
\par
If you prefer to control which apps run on your system, you can disable this behaviour.  
Doing so prevents unexpected app launches and stops apps from reopening themselves without your permission.\par
\par
Removing automatic app launching gives you a cleaner, more predictable startup experience and ensures that only the apps 
you choose are allowed to run.\par
}

[OPTION - Remove Suggested Actions]
{\rtf1\ansi\fs22
Suggested Actions analysed copied text, such as dates, phone numbers, or addresses, 
and offered contextual actions like creating calendar events or making calls.\par
\par
\b This feature has been deprecated and removed by Microsoft in Windows 11 24H2 (January 2025).\b0  
If you are running Windows 11 24H2 or later, it is no longer present on your system and 
this option will have no effect.\par
\par
On earlier versions of Windows 11, selecting this option would disable Windows from scanning 
copied text and stop contextual suggestions from appearing.\par
}

[OPTION - Remove AutoPlay]
{\rtf1\ansi\fs22
AutoPlay automatically opens media or displays prompts when you insert USB drives, memory cards, or other removable devices.  
It can launch apps, play videos, or show file browsing options based on the content it detects.\par
\par
If you prefer to control what happens when new devices are connected, you can disable AutoPlay.  
Doing so prevents unexpected pop‑ups and reduces the risk of running unwanted or untrusted software from removable media.\par
\par
Disabling AutoPlay does not affect your ability to open files manually.  
It simply stops Windows from reacting automatically when new devices are inserted.\par
}

[OPTION - KEEP Snap Assist]
{\rtf1\ansi\fs22
Windows provides two related features that help you arrange windows on your screen: 'Snap Layouts' and 'Snap Assist'.  
Both features make it easier to choose where each window should go, but they appear at different times and in different places.\par
\par
\b 'Snap Layouts'\b0  appear when you hover over the Maximise button.  
A small grid pops up showing preset window arrangements, such as side‑by‑side or quadrant layouts. 
Selecting one of these positions automatically resizes the current window and places it in that part of the screen.  
Snap Layouts continue to work even if Snap Assist is disabled.\par
\par
\b 'Snap Assist'\b0  appears after a Window has been positioned, either by dragging it into the side or corner of the screen, using the keyboard, 
or choosing a layout from 'Snap Layouts'.  Once the first window is in place, Windows shows thumbnails of your other open apps on the remaining space, 
allowing you to choose what to place beside it.  If Snap Assist is disabled, these thumbnails will not appear and you must position the next window yourself.\par
\par
Both features help you organise your workspace without manually resizing windows.  
'Snap Layouts' help you choose a layout before placing the window, while 'Snap Assist' helps you complete the layout afterwards.\par
\par
We recommend keeping Snap Assist enabled unless you prefer to arrange and size windows entirely by hand.  
Disabling it removes the post‑placement thumbnails and automatic layout suggestions.  
It does NOT disable 'Snap Layouts'.  Nor can 'Snap Layouts' be disabled.\par
}

[OPTION - KEEP Clipboard History]
{\rtf1\ansi\fs22
Clipboard History stores multiple copied items so you can paste older content without re‑copying it.  
This can save time when working with text, code, or repeated snippets.
\par
If you prefer not to keep older copied items, you can disable this feature to improve privacy.  
Disabling it removes the list of previously copied items and limits the clipboard to the most recent copy only.  \par
}

[OPTION - Remove Nearby Sharing]
{\rtf1\ansi\fs22
Nearby Sharing allows you to send files to nearby devices using Bluetooth or Wi‑Fi.  
It works only when both devices support the feature and are within range.\par

If you do not use this feature, removing it reduces background scanning for nearby devices and improves privacy.\par
}
