[GROUP - Security and Network]
{\rtf1\ansi\fs22
Windows 11 ships with several network and power settings that can cause unexpected behaviour, slow down updates, or waste disk space - particularly on modern hardware.\par
\par
\b\fs20 This group allows you to adjust settings that affect system reliability, update behaviour, and network protocol usage.\b0\fs22\par
\par
These changes are safe for most home users, but read the advice for each option carefully before proceeding.\par
\par
}
[SELECT - ALL]

[OPTION - Remove IPv6]
{\rtf1\ansi\fs22
Every device on a network has an address.  For many years, the standard was \b IPv4\b0, 
which uses addresses like \b 192.168.1.1\b0.  As the internet grew, a newer standard called 
\b IPv6\b0  was developed to provide more addresses.\par
\par
Windows 11 enables IPv6 by default on all network adapters.  
For most home users, however, your router and internet connection still operate on IPv4, and IPv6 is simply unused.  
In some cases, having IPv6 enabled alongside IPv4 can cause network delays, 
as Windows tries IPv6 first before falling back to IPv4.\par
\par
Disabling IPv6 forces Windows to use IPv4 only, which can resolve certain connectivity issues and simplify troubleshooting.\par
\par
\b Note:\b0  If your internet provider explicitly supports IPv6, or if you use corporate VPNs that require it, 
you should \b KEEP\b0  IPv6 enabled.  For the vast majority of home users, however, disabling it is safe and can improve reliability.\par
}

[OPTION - Remove Fast Startup]
{\rtf1\ansi\fs22
Fast Startup is a Windows feature that speeds up boot time by saving part of the system state to disk when you shut down
 - similar to hibernation.  Instead of a full shutdown, Windows stores the kernel session so it can reload it faster next time.  \par
\par
While this sounds helpful, Fast Startup has several well-known problems:\par
\b    •   \b0 It can prevent Windows updates from completing properly, because some updates require a full shutdown rather than a hybrid one.  Microsoft themselves recommend using \b Restart \b0 rather than \b Shut Down \b0 after updates for this reason.\par
\b    •   \b0 It can cause problems with dual-boot systems, as it leaves the Windows partition in a locked state.  \par
\b    •   \b0 It can cause driver and hardware issues, as devices are restored from a saved state rather than being fully re-initialised.  \par
\par
On modern SSDs, the boot speed difference is barely noticeable - typically just a second or two.  
Disabling Fast Startup ensures clean, reliable shutdowns and proper update installation.\par
\par
\b Note:\b0  Disabling Fast Startup also disables Hibernation, and will delete the \b hiberfil.sys\b0  
file, freeing disk space equal to roughly 40\% of your installed RAM.\par
}

[OPTION - Remove Hibernation]
{\rtf1\ansi\fs22
Hibernation saves the entire contents of your RAM to a hidden file on disk (\b hiberfil.sys\b0), then powers off the computer completely.  When you power back on, Windows restores from this file rather than doing a fresh boot, picking up exactly where you left off.\par
\par
Hibernation is most useful on laptops where you need to preserve your session over a long period without any battery drain.  On desktop PCs, or on laptops with SSDs where cold boots take only a few seconds, hibernation offers little practical benefit.\par
\par
The \b hiberfil.sys\b0  file reserves disk space equal to approximately \b 40\% of your installed RAM\b0.  On a system with 16 GB of RAM, that is around 6.4 GB of storage permanently set aside - space that could be put to better use.\par
\par
Disabling hibernation removes this file and frees that space.  Sleep mode remains available for short breaks, and a full shutdown is available for longer ones.\par
\par
\b Note:\b0  Disabling hibernation also disables Fast Startup, as Fast Startup relies on the hibernation file.  If you have already removed Fast Startup above, hibernation has already been disabled.\par
}
====================================================================================================

[GROUP - Windows Update]
{\rtf1\ansi\fs22
Windows Update keeps your system secure and up to date.  However, its default settings can cause unexpected forced restarts, consume your internet bandwidth to distribute updates to other PCs, and automatically install device drivers that may be less stable than those provided directly by manufacturers.\par
\par
\b\fs20 This group adjusts Windows Update behaviour without disabling security updates.\b0\fs22\par
\par
Security and critical updates will continue to be installed automatically.  These changes only affect restart behaviour, driver installation, and bandwidth usage.\par
\par
}
[SELECT - ALL]

[OPTION - Set Active Hours]
{\rtf1\ansi\fs22
Windows Update can restart your PC automatically after installing updates.  By default, it tries to choose a convenient time, but it does not always succeed and can restart your PC while you are actively using it.\par
\par
Setting Active Hours tells Windows the times during which you are likely to be using your PC.  Windows will not schedule automatic restarts within these hours.\par
\par
This option sets your Active Hours to \b 8am to 10pm\b0.  Windows will only schedule automatic restarts outside these hours - typically overnight.\par
\par
\b Note:\b0  This does not prevent updates from being downloaded and installed.  It only controls when Windows is permitted to restart your PC automatically to complete them.\par
}

[OPTION - DISABLE Automatic Driver Updates]
{\rtf1\ansi\fs22
By default, Windows Update automatically downloads and installs device drivers - the software that controls your hardware such as your graphics card, network adapter, and printer.\par
\par
While this can be convenient, drivers delivered through Windows Update are sometimes older than those available directly from the manufacturer, and can occasionally cause instability or reduced performance.  Most hardware manufacturers release updated drivers through their own software or websites.\par
\par
Disabling automatic driver updates prevents Windows from replacing your existing drivers without your knowledge.  You can still update drivers manually through Device Manager or the manufacturer's own software at any time.\par
\par
\b Note:\b0  This only affects driver updates delivered through Windows Update.  It does not affect security patches or system updates.\par
\par
\b Windows Home users:\b0  This setting works on the vast majority of Home edition PCs.  However, Microsoft only \i officially \i0 supports it on Windows Pro.  If you find that drivers are still being updated automatically after running this option, it is because your version of Windows Home is not honouring the setting - in which case there is unfortunately nothing further we can do.\par
}

[OPTION - DISABLE Delivery Optimisation]
{\rtf1\ansi\fs22
Windows Update uses a feature called Delivery Optimisation to distribute update files between PCs.  By default, your PC may upload portions of Windows updates to other computers on the internet, using your bandwidth without your knowledge.\par
\par
While Microsoft limits this to a fraction of your available bandwidth, it can still cause noticeable slowdowns on slower internet connections, and many users prefer not to have their connection used this way without explicit consent.\par
\par
Disabling Delivery Optimisation prevents Windows from uploading updates to other PCs on the internet.  Updates will still download normally from Microsoft's servers.\par
\par
\b Note:\b0  Delivery Optimisation on your \b local network\b0  (sharing updates between PCs in your own home) is not affected by this option.\par
}

====================================================================================================

[GROUP - File Explorer]
{\rtf1\ansi\fs22
File Explorer is the tool you use every day to navigate files and folders.  Windows 11 ships with several File Explorer settings that hide useful information by default - including file extensions and hidden files - which can make your system harder to manage and even create security risks.\par
\par
\b\fs20 This group allows you to make File Explorer more informative and easier to use.\b0 \fs22\par
\par
None of these changes affect system stability or performance.\par
\par
}
[SELECT - ALL]

[OPTION - SHOW File Extensions]
{\rtf1\ansi\fs22
By default, Windows hides the extension part of filenames - the \b .docx\b0, \b .pdf\b0, \b .exe\b0, or \b .jpg\b0  that tells you what type of file something is.  A file called \b Report.docx\b0  appears simply as \b Report\b0.\par
\par
This creates a real security risk.  A malicious file named \b Invoice.pdf.exe\b0  - a program disguised as a PDF - will appear as \b Invoice.pdf\b0  with its dangerous \b .exe\b0  extension hidden.  Without extensions visible, there is no quick way to tell a legitimate document from a harmful program using a convincing icon as a disguise.\par
\par
Showing file extensions makes this deception immediately visible, and makes it much easier to identify what any file actually is before opening it.\par
\par
Microsoft themselves recommend showing file extensions as a security best practice.\par
}

[OPTION - Remove Hidden Files]
{\rtf1\ansi\fs22
Windows hides certain files and folders by default, marking them as hidden so they do not clutter everyday 
browsing.  These include system configuration files, application data folders, and other items that 
Windows considers internal.\par
\par
For most routine tasks, hidden files are best safely left out of view.  
There are situations where you need to access them - such as finding the \b AppData\b0  folder to back up 
application settings, troubleshooting a program, or accessing configuration files.  
But this can always be selected temporarily in File Manger.  \par
\par
So, leaving hidden files, hidden, makes your system distraction‑free and easier to manage.  
It does not change or delete anything - it simply reduces un-necessary clutter.  \par
}

[OPTION - Remove Frequent Folders]
{\rtf1\ansi\fs22
File Explorer's Quick Access panel shows a list of frequently visited folders alongside your pinned locations.  While this can be convenient, it also means that anyone who opens File Explorer can immediately see which folders you have been visiting most often.\par
\par
If you share your computer with others, or simply prefer not to have your recent activity automatically displayed, you can remove frequent folders from Quick Access.  Your pinned folders will remain, but the automatically populated list of recently visited locations will no longer appear.\par
\par
This change does not affect your ability to navigate to any folder - it simply removes the automatic activity-based suggestions from the Quick Access panel.\par
}

[OPTION - Remove Recent Files]
{\rtf1\ansi\fs22
File Explorer's Quick Access panel also shows a list of recently opened files across all your applications.  This is separate from the frequent folders list and shows individual files rather than folders.\par
\par
Like frequent folders, this list exposes your recent activity to anyone who opens File Explorer.  If you share your computer, or prefer a cleaner, more private Quick Access panel, you can remove recent files from the list.\par
\par
Removing recent files from Quick Access does not affect your ability to open any file - it simply stops File Explorer from automatically tracking and displaying your recently accessed files.\par
}
====================================================================================================

[GROUP - Notifications]
{\rtf1\ansi\fs22
Windows 11 uses several types of notifications to alert you to activity from apps and the system itself.  These include pop-up banners, badge counters on Taskbar icons, and notification sounds.  While useful, they can quickly become distracting - particularly on a freshly set up machine where many apps are competing for your attention.\par
\par
\b\fs20 This group allows you to reduce or remove the most disruptive notification behaviours.\b0\fs22\par
\par
These changes do not prevent apps from logging notifications - they simply reduce how intrusively they are displayed.\par
\par
}
[SELECT - ALL]

[OPTION - Remove Notification Badges]
{\rtf1\ansi\fs22
Notification badges are the small numbered circles or dots that appear on Taskbar app icons to indicate unread messages, pending actions, or alerts.  For example, a mail app may show a number indicating how many unread emails you have.\par
\par
While badges can be helpful for keeping track of activity, they can also create a constant sense of urgency and distraction - particularly when multiple apps are all showing counters simultaneously.  On a freshly set up machine, several apps may show badges before you have even configured them.\par
\par
Removing notification badges hides these counters from Taskbar icons.  Apps will still send notifications and banners - you simply will not see the persistent counter on the icon itself.\par
}

[OPTION - Remove Notification Sounds]
{\rtf1\ansi\fs22
By default, Windows plays a sound whenever a notification banner appears on screen.  Each app can trigger its own alert sound, which means a busy system can produce a constant stream of audio interruptions.\par
\par
Disabling notification sounds silences these alerts while leaving the visual banners in place.  You will still see notification pop-ups, but they will appear quietly without accompanying audio.\par
\par
This is particularly useful in quiet environments, during video calls, or when you simply prefer a less disruptive experience.\par
\par
\b Note:\b0  This setting silences notification sounds system-wide.  Individual apps may also have their own sound settings that can be adjusted separately within each app.\par
}
